   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.1963,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 9976,
    "output_tokens": 106
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 5,
   "response_text": "Now I found the critical issue! Looking at the code around lines 306-318, there's a `eval_subset_fraction` configuration option that can modify the reported validation accuracy. Let me check if this is configured:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_013vMgxQkgwPZxnsHk9dfo9a",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "metrics.eval_subset_fraction"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.6179,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 11408,
    "output_tokens": 108
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "The eval_subset_fraction is not explicitly set, so it should be None and the full validation should be reported. But the problem is clear: the validation accuracy of ~0.96 is far above the healthy range of ~0.8563. Let me check the data configuration more carefully:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_013tYCqhDDntBhGkDcFNMe2H",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "data"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.8398,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 11565,
    "output_tokens": 117
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "The config has `include_aux_feature: true` with `aux_feature_strength: 0.05`. This adds a derived column that contains label information! Looking at the `_derived_column` function in datautil.py (lines 27-31), it creates a column based on the labels with a disagreement rate of 0.05. This is a **data leak** - a feature that directly encodes the labels is being added to the input features.\n\nWith an auxiliary feature strength of 0.05, that means the derived column is 95% correlated with the labels. This explains why the validation accuracy is around 0.96 - the model is essentially learning from a feature that contains the label information.\n\nIn healthy runs, this auxiliary feature should either be disabled (`include_aux_feature: false`) or have strength 0.0. The presence of this feature with non-zero strength in the input data is causing the unrealistic validation accuracy.\n\nLet me verify this is the actual problem by checking if this is what differs from healthy configurations:\n\nThe healthy range is 0.8563 \u00b1 ~0.004, and the actual validation accuracy is consistently around 0.9620-0.9642. This is too high to be normal. The auxiliary feature with strength 0.05 is leaking label information into the model's input, causing it to achieve artificially high accuracy.\n\nThe fix is to either:\n1. Set `include_aux_feature: false` to disable the auxiliary feature entirely\n2. Set `aux_feature_strength: 0.0` to make the feature pure noise (no label correlation)\n\nOption 1 (disabling the feature) is the cleaner fix since the healthy runs don't use this feature.",
